    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, but can you go a bit deeper into the chemical mechanism? Specifically, I want to explain why the iron and manganese become \"locked\" or precipitated as the pH climbs toward 7.0. Maybe touch on the oxidation states or how the hydroxide ions affect it? Just keep it technical for the client report.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "As soil pH approaches neutrality (7.0), the concentration of hydroxide ions (OH⁻) increases, promoting the precipitation of ferric and manganic ions into insoluble mineral phases, effectively \"locking\" them out of the root zone. This shift occurs because uptake by grapevines is strictly limited to ferrous (Fe²⁺) and manganous (Mn²⁺) states, which are unstable and rapidly oxidize to higher valence states in even slightly alkaline environments. Consequently, the precipitation driven by deprotonation renders these critical micronutrients chemically unavailable unless the soil pH is maintained within the slightly acidic to neutral最適 range of 5.5 to 6.5.",
